--TEST--
A call on a variable whose type can never be a closure does not compile, which refuses PHP's variable function — `rule:types/callable-values`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/callable-values`: only a closure is callable, so a
// `string` holding a function's name is refused where it is called.
string $name = "Zoë";
string $function = 'strlen';
echo $function($name), "\n";
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0420]: a value of type `string` is not callable
  --> case.nvs:6:6
  |
6 | echo $function($name), "\n";
  |      ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ called with `(...)` here
  = help: only an anonymous function or a method reference can be called. Write `fn (...) => ...`, or `Class::method(...)` or `$obj->method(...)`. A string with a function's name cannot be called

